A||||10||BluByYou||'87 GT top speed
Z||000000||BluByYou||08-27-99||07:11 AM||||I was reading some of the current posts and came across the one from Cutter on passing a dude at 120 mph.  I too have an 87 GT, auto, and (you may have guessed) blue in color!  Now mine has 52000 miles, new tires, VERY well maintained.  I'm the second owner and the first was a girl.  Long story short, 120 in my car would be dangerous.  I've hit 110, and it gets a bit "floaty" - the sensation feels like I get wind lift.  Up to 90 or so it's great, but I wouldn't touch 120.  Is something wrong with mine, or is this pretty common?
Z||000001||bHooper||08-27-99||09:06 AM||||When my 86.5 was running, I had it to 115 mph<br>several times.  I never noticed any "lift" effect.  In fact, it always felt as if the faster I went, the more the car tucked to the <br>asphalt.  Doew your car have a rear spoiler?  I have always understood, and believed, that  the spoiler on these cars becomes very usefull at highway speeds and above.  
Z||000002||BluByYou||08-27-99||09:48 AM||||Thanks for the input.  Yep - I have the rear spoiler.  Which makes it even harder to understand why it acts that way in it's present condition.  A friend had an '88 Formula and spoke of it's high speed abilities much differently than I would talk about mine.  He talked about s-curves at 80 mph plus and so on.  Hence the question:  Is it an '87 and earlier thing, or just mine?  According to you, just mine. (?)  
Z||000003||lowCG||08-27-99||10:34 AM||||Stock springs and shocks are for getting groceries,you'll need much different stuff for serious driving.
Z||000004||SCCA FIERO||08-27-99||02:00 PM||||I think I read somewhere that the stock Fiero has a 90lb uplift at 100mph. If you look at the radiator, when the hood is closed there is no where for the air to go. The air passes through the radiator and then hits a steel wall. I think some kind of scoop is in order to let the air escape to prevent the "pillow" of air that makes your car feel "floaty". I would seriously consider this if you want to travel at that speed (on deserted roads or closed courses of course) and be safe.
Z||000005||ChadMan||08-27-99||03:32 PM||||I heard that 90 lb figure too - I think it was in one of the initial road tests of the 84 Fiero by Road & Track or Car and Driver.  I wonder if the aero nose helps or hurts the problem (you'd think it would help!)<p>In a car that only has about 1100 lbs on the front wheels in the first place, a 90 lb lift is pretty significant!
Z||000006||87GT||08-27-99||08:41 PM||||Hey BBY, my car also feels a bit floaty at high speeds also.  The original owner told me that he has had the car up over 130 mph.  The fastest I have gone is 105.  It didnt feel to stable at that speed.  
Z||000007||RFMike||08-27-99||10:41 PM||||I have an 861/2 GT, and have had it between  125 and 130.  I'm not sure how fast- the speedo pegs at about 128.  I don't know how you somebody can know how fast they are going beyond the peg, other than be rpm calcs.<br>At 110 it is still stable.  At 115, it is still accelerating, but feels loose. At 125, I didn't feel comfortable taking my eyes off the road because of the floating sensation.  When I looked at the speedo and saw it was pegged, I backed off.  I could have gone maybe a little faster, but not without some major work on suspension, steering, and maybe aerodynamics.  <br>By the way, this was on a flat state highway, able to see ten miles, no cars in sight, and one or two houses in the whole stretch.  The only way to go that fast on public highways.
Z||000008||JimiZ||08-28-99||12:50 AM||||In my opinion, you should look into some new shocks and struts.  Also, check your front swaybar links and make sure none are broken or missing.  Check into getting a rear swaybar if you don't have one.  Get some stiffer springs.  <br>My friend Rick's car felt very loose when he first got it.  we spent a couple saturdays replacing the front bushings with urethane types, also the front swaybar bushings, put in new ball joints, and new shocks.  You can't imagine the difference that will make.<br>The steering is so tight now, the car is like a go-kart. It has almost mental-telepathic steering, think it and it does it.  Unfortunately, we haven't replaced any components yet on the rear suspension.  It's going to get the same treatment as the front, but we won't do it untill we yank the engine for a re-build/mod.  <br>If yer on a budget, try shocks and struts first.  Then proceed.<br>Think it, and it goes there.
Z||000009||batboy||08-28-99||06:34 AM||||Jimi and LowCG speak wisdom about the need to upgrade things if you want to go fast. Heavier duty front springs and performance shocks will help some. Don't forget about having good high speed rated tires too.<p>One of the best things you can do to improve front end stability at high speed is to invest in a good front air dam. A rear spoiler doesn't do anything for the front end and actually probably not really much for the back end either.
Z||000010||terryk||08-29-99||01:27 PM||||The speed limiter in the 7170 ECM (86-88) V8 is set for 124 MPH off and 100 MPH on.<p>Odds are you could hit that.<p>
